{
  "id": "delete-the-middle-node-of-a-linked-list",
  "questionId": "delete-the-middle-node-of-a-linked-list",
  "slug": "delete-the-middle-node-of-a-linked-list",
  "title": "Delete the Middle Node of a Linked List",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Fast slow pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "fast & slow pointers",
    "linked list",
    "two pointers"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "you could count the length up front, but can you both find and remove the middle node in a single pass?",
    "use the classic fast/slow pointer walk to locate the middle, but you'll also need to know the node right before it in order to unlink it",
    "keep a third pointer (or just remember the previous node) that trails one step behind slow as you advance",
    "when fast reaches the end, the trailing pointer's next node is the middle - skip over it by linking the trailing pointer directly to the node after the middle"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "getting the even-length middle index wrong and deleting the first of the two center nodes instead of the second",
    "not handling the single-node case, which should produce an empty list",
    "losing track of the node before the middle, making it impossible to unlink the middle node directly"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A singly linked list is given to you as an array `nums`, where `nums[i]`\nis the value of the i-th node in order.\n\n**Delete the middle node and return the resulting list's values in order.**\n\nIf the list has an even number of nodes, treat the middle as the second of the two center nodes (index n/2 using integer division) and delete that one.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 10^5"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node list (deleting it leaves an empty list)",
      "a two-node list",
      "even-length vs odd-length lists"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 3, 4, 7, 1, 2, 6]",
        "output": "[1, 3, 4, 1, 2, 6]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 3, 4, 1, 2, 6]",
        "explanation": "7 nodes, middle index 3 (value 7) is removed"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]",
        "output": "[1, 2, 4]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 2, 4]",
        "explanation": "4 nodes, n/2 = 2, so index 2 (value 3) is removed"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1]",
        "output": "[]",
        "expectedOutput": "[]",
        "explanation": "a single node is its own middle, deleting it leaves an empty list"
      }
